**Binding architecture doc**: `docs/rules-idioms-architecture/fs3-architecture.md` — the five rules, the crate graph, and the refused-anti-pattern list reviews cite by name. This section instantiates pij-team TENETS 1-6 in THIS repo's stack; it does not restate them.

**Local mechanisms.** Boundaries are crates, enforced by `crates/testkit/arch-allowlist.toml` and checked by `crates/testkit/src/bin/arch_check.rs` inside `harness checks` — an undeclared dependency is a build/CI error, and an allowlist edit is ONE deliberate granted line, never improvised. Functional core / imperative shell: `crates/core` is pure data-in/data-out with no tokio, sqlx or HTTP. The composition root is `AppState::from_config` (crates/daemon/src/wiring.rs:116) — that config match IS the IoC container. Fakes over mocks, shipped from `crates/testkit`; mocking frameworks are refused by name. Placement rule: pure logic → core · world→types → parsers · port impl → providers · PG → store · reads git → git · wires or serves → daemon.

**The central architectural bet of this plan, stated so a reviewer can attack it.** A ddoc row is modelled as an `Element` in the EXISTING element tree, not as a parallel document type in a parallel table. That single decision buys the whole downstream pipeline unchanged — content-hash dedupe, the embed and summarize queue, similarity search, `get`, `tree` — and reduces this plan to: one new element kind, one additive typed field, one parser, one adapter, and the surfacing. The alternative (a dedicated `ddoc_rows` table) would force a second implementation of embedding, search and retrieval, roughly doubling the plan. The bet is proven or refuted in wave 1, first, by u1+u3 — not discovered at composition.

**Two refusals, both load-bearing.** (1) NO new port trait. Architecture rule 3 — a trait earns its existence only when a second real implementation exists or is firmly planned — and a subprocess call does not qualify. The `ddocs` integration is a pure envelope parser in core plus a thin spawn in the daemon; the "fake" in tests is a recorded envelope, which is a better double than a trait would have given us. (2) NO reimplementation of dd's schema resolution or link resolution. dd's four-root schema ladder and typed-link semantics are where a Rust reimplementation silently diverges; we shell out and stamp the version we shelled out to.

**What this plan touches.** core: new module + one enum variant + one struct field (WAVE 0, PM-authored, frozen before fan-out). parsers: new `ddoc` module + a discovery admission branch. store: one forward migration + metadata persistence + filters + the inverse index. daemon: new `ddoc` adapter module + scan-path dispatch + search/read surfacing. cli: one bundled doc page + search flags. testkit: fixtures. No crate gains a dependency on a crate it did not already depend on; `crates/parsers` gains the `serde_json` external, which is ONE new allowlist row under `[crates.fs3-parsers] external` and is granted here, in writing, by the PM.

**Schema/migration ownership.** Exactly one unit (u3) owns `crates/store/migrations/` and adds exactly one forward migration. No other unit writes SQL. This is deliberate: two units writing migrations is an unorderable merge.

**Wave 0 is the contracts phase and it is PM-authored.** Tenet 2 says freeze the seams before you spawn anyone, and tenet 5 says freeze the contract WITH its proof. The types below land as one commit on `008-ddocs-scan`, with their own unit tests green and `harness checks` green, BEFORE any coder branches. Every coder branches from that commit. A unit that needs one of these types widened raises it as a PM ruling; it does not edit the contract.

**WAVE 0 — the frozen contract, PM-authored, verbatim. Coders code against this; they do not change it.**

